  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe use a gradient-free optimization method, CMA-ES, to search for the minimal overlap. The core idea is to parameterize the symmetric step function by its first half, ensuring integral constraints via repair, and optimize the maximum overlap through a repair-based objective function. This method handles non-linear constraints and has demonstrated effectiveness in continuous optimization. The key innovations are:\n- Using integral constraint handling via repair scaling to maintain constraints\n- Employing CMA-ES's natural handling of box constraints for [0,1] values\n- Providing a repair handler within the objective function to keep solutions feasible\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nRepresentation:\n- Step function h(x) represented by vector h_opt of length n_half\n- Full function h(x) is h_opt mirrored to [1,2]\nConstraints:\n- Integral constraint: \u2211h_opt[i] = n_points/4\n- Box constraints: 0 \u2264 h_opt[i] \u2264 1 for all i\nObjective:\n- Compute maximum overlap via correlation: \n  C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Implemented via: dx * max(np.correlate(h_full, 1-h_full, mode='full'))\n\nDerivation for integral constraint:\n- Total integral over [0,2] is 1\n- Reflecting symmetric h(x) gives \u222b\u2080\u00b2h(x)dx = 2*\u222b\u2080\u00b9h(x)dx = 1\n- Thus \u222b\u2080\u00b9h(x)dx = 0.5\n- With dx=2/n_points, sum(h_opt) must = (0.5) / dx = n_points/4\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n1. Setup\n   - Define n_points=280, n_half=140\n   - Set dx_val=2.0/n_points\n   - Set integral_target=n_points/4\n2. Helper Functions\n   - Define compute_overlap(h_opt):\n        h_full = np.concatenate([h_opt,h_opt[::-1]])\n        corr = np.correlate(h_full,1-h_full,mode='full')\n        return dx_val * np.max(corr)\n   - Define repair(h, integral_target):\n        current_sum = np.sum(h)\n        if current_sum \u2248 0: return np.zeros_like(h)\n        scaling_factor = integral_target/current_sum\n        return np.clip(h * scaling_factor, 0, 1)\n3. Optimization\n   - Use CMA-ES with mean=initial_h_values[:n_half] if available\n   - Bounds: (0.0, 1.0) for all dimensions\n   - Options:\n        maxiter=2000\n        seed=seed\n        ftol=1e-10\n        gtol=1e-10\n4. Cleanup\n   - Build full vector: np.concatenate([best_x, best_x[::-1]])\n   - Return tuple with solution, dx_val, and n_points count\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Base Configuration: \n   - n_points=280 (higher fidelity for better solutions)\n   - Initial mean set to parent's first half if available\n   - sigma0=0.1 (reasonable initial standard deviation)\n- Key Variants:\n  - Hyperparameter Sweep: \n    * Run with combination: (sigma0=0.2, n_points=70)\n    * Run with combination: (sigma0=0.1, n_points=35)\n    * Run with combination: (sigma0=0.05, n_points=56)\n  - Gradient-based refinement:\n    * If time remaining: use SLSQP from scipy with bounds (optional)\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Dominant cost: 2000 objective evaluations\n- Each evaluation: O(140^2) correlation (\u2248 78400 operations)\n- Repair: O(140) operations\n- Robustness:\n  - If initial_h_values missing: use np.random.rand(n_half)\n  - If time budget exhausted: return best solution found\n  - Catch NaNs in integral_target repair\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Pre-check candidate vector sum: must \u2248 n_points/4\n- Post-optimization: recompute overlap to ensure consistency\n- Return tuple (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) where:\n   h_values shape = (n_points,)\n   type = float32\n   sum(h_values) / (dx_val*2) \u2248 1\n- Validation passes with:\n   import pytest\n   assert 0.36 \u2264 c5_bound < 0.381277",
